# PART 6 — REAL-TIME PROPERTY EDITING PIPELINE

## 17. Implement one authoritative update path

All inspector changes must follow a controlled data pipeline:

Admin selects component
→ Inspector reads its configuration
→ User changes a supported property
→ Validate the proposed value
→ Apply an immutable PageDocument update
→ Resolve responsive styling
→ Render through the existing component adapter
→ Actual canvas changes
→ Mark working draft dirty
→ Save through existing persistence when requested.

Do not directly mutate DOM styles as the only source of truth.

Direct DOM style changes may be used for transient interactions in later phases, but committed values must exist in PageDocument.

## 18. Separate transient input from committed values

Numeric inputs and color fields may contain intermediate values while the administrator is typing.

For example, entering `120` may temporarily produce an input containing `1` or `12`.

Do not reject every intermediate keystroke by resetting the field.

Use temporary control state where appropriate.

Validate and commit at suitable boundaries.

For controls designed for immediate feedback, use safe incremental updates.

Do not send a database request for every keystroke.

## 19. Validate property updates centrally

Before accepting a property change:

- Check node existence.
- Check effective lock state.
- Check trusted capabilities.
- Check property support.
- Check value type.
- Check allowed unit.
- Check numeric limits.
- Check responsive breakpoint.
- Check data-binding boundaries.

Return structured errors.

Do not silently ignore invalid values.

## 20. Respect locked components

A locked component or a component under a locked ancestor must not be modified through the inspector.

The inspector may show values in read-only mode.

The lock behavior must agree with Prompt 11's structural command system.

