# PART 21 — PERFORMANCE-OPTIMIZED PROPERTY EDITING

## 70. Use localized immutable updates

Changing one property must not deep-clone the entire document unnecessarily.

Use existing tree utilities and structural sharing.

Avoid changing unrelated node identities.

Do not use JSON serialization as the default state update mechanism.

## 71. Keep interactive inputs responsive

For sliders and rapidly changing values:

- Use appropriate local control state.
- Batch updates where needed.
- Avoid expensive validation on every pointer event.
- Avoid unnecessary layout measurement.
- Validate committed values.
- Keep the actual canvas responsive.

Do not send a Supabase request for every slider movement.

## 72. Separate lightweight and expensive work

Lightweight:
- Current control value.
- Selected node lookup.
- Simple validated property update.

Potentially expensive:
- Full-document validation.
- Large schema serialization.
- Database save.
- Heavy layout measurement.
- Extensive image rendering.

Schedule expensive operations at appropriate boundaries.

## 73. Preserve public bundle isolation

The normal public storefront must not download:

- Color picker UI.
- Inspector form controls.
- Editor panel components.
- Editor-only state managers.
- Property control metadata that is only needed for editing.
- Future drag-and-drop libraries.

Public code should import only the shared rendering and configuration functionality it genuinely needs.

